import{_ as i,P as n,d as a,e as r,x as o,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position","xkChars":0,"xkWords":454,"xkLessons":{"D.02":{"title":"D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Positions","lead":"Understanding someone’s experience, proving a formula, establishing what happened on a particular day, and deciding what is fair cannot be replaced by the same predictive leaderboard. Careful listening and careful verification can proceed together: correcting a date does not cancel someone’s standing to tell their story.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 4631–4646; original anchor section-d-02; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “Being useful, actionable, consensually accepted, and true are not the same standard.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","This section first distinguishes the burdens of proof of different epistemic activities. Formal derivation must disclose premises and rules; empirical judgment must explain how observations support claims; interpretive research must examine texts, contexts, and competing readings together; normative argument must state reasons and affected positions. Practical and embodied experience may also provide irreplaceable materials about certain circumstances and skills, but cannot automatically answer every causal or normative question. The shared requirement is to make grounds and limits visible, not to reduce all knowledge to prediction scores.","Falsification, paradigms, and research programmes are listed as entry points for criticism at different scales; they should not be combined into a single rule that every counterexample overturns everything. One failed local test may concern auxiliary assumptions, object definitions, measurement, or a broader theory. Examine what the original claim promised, which part the materials touch, and which subsequent judgments depend on it. The possibility of local revision does not mean patches may forever protect the core; when evidence sufficiently touches shared premises, it cannot be dismissed as a small error either.","Changing a question can create valuable new research, but the task change must be public. An original prediction says an indicator will decline, but it does not; later, the question shifts to how people understand that indicator and produces rich interviews. The new interpretive study may hold, but cannot retrospectively turn the old prediction into a hit. Usefulness means assistance for a particular use; actionability means an action plan can be formed from it; consensus means participants reach some agreement; truth concerns whether the corresponding factual proposition holds. The four may be related, but do not define one another.","Language sometimes does more than report facts: under appropriate conditions it constitutes commitments, naming, or granting eligibility. Reports require factual grounds; commitments concern what speakers undertake; granting eligibility involves authorized positions and rules; naming also requires distinguishing an analyst’s introduction of terminology from an actual change in institutional identity. Between uttering a sentence and effectively completing the action, check the speaker’s standing, context, rules, and relevant conditions; literal recognition alone is insufficient.","“I agree” may be a person’s genuine expression, yet still fail to constitute valid authorization under particular pressures, insufficient information, or unmet permission conditions. Understanding the utterance’s meaning and checking institutional conditions are separate tasks; a string cannot directly generate permission. Language mismatch may also stem from translation, terminology, context, or communication-channel differences; it cannot directly diagnose personality or intelligence. Discussing specific language difficulties requires corresponding professional materials and protection, without inferring a personal defect from inability to understand.","Epistemic positions affect access to materials, questions that can be raised, and who is more readily believed. Some people bearing consequences may understand everyday barriers better yet lack opportunities for safe expression and recording. Better listening, translation, and safe entry points can bring previously invisible materials into analysis, but each claim must be verified: excluded people must neither be dismissed a priori nor granted automatic truth privileges for every factual statement. Respecting a subject’s standing and judging factual truth are responsibilities that can be fulfilled together.","Protective non-disclosure can coexist with trustworthy verification. For example, restricted materials may be given to trusted reviewers within a justified scope, while the strength of public conclusions about portions unavailable for public verification is reduced. One must neither require every sensitive detail to be public before acknowledging a problem, nor use protection to waive all verification or expand conclusions without limit. This section connects to §16.6 through safe expression and traceable review, not encouragement of concealment and unchallengeable claims.","Mutual correction among methods must still preserve real differences in theoretical commitments. The influence of position on knowledge production does not imply that objects are entirely fabricated by arbitrary narratives; reasons may enter causal explanation without eliminating the task of understanding meaning. Reliable date records may correct a remembered date, while why the speaker links an experience to a moment and what it means to them remain researchable. Recognizing their standing to tell the story does not guarantee every date is accurate; correcting a date does not establish that all their experience or expression has lost value.","The eight research entries concern the grounds of experience and reasoning, standards of scientific criticism, the relationship between practice and problem-solving, objects and construction, embodiment and lived experience, textual context and understanding, language and concepts in relation to action conditions, and positions and exclusion in knowledge production. This section lists Hume, Descartes, Popper, Kuhn, Lakatos, Peirce, James, Dewey, Husserl, Heidegger, Merleau-Ponty, Dilthey, Gadamer, Ricoeur, Wittgenstein, Austin, and others as entry points. It does not claim that they agree, or that this text has reconstructed their complete original works. Detailed comparisons of particular authors still require corresponding editions and source texts; a list of names cannot fill an argument.","The strongest objection is that preserving multiple responsibilities for knowledge makes every claim acceptable. The response is that different methods do not mean no standards: criticism should address the actual claim. Empirical facts require material; formal inferences require premises and rules; interpretation requires textual context and competing readings; and normative claims require reasons and affected positions. Another objection accepts only predictable answers, which would unjustifiably erase grounded textual understanding and normative disagreement. Predictive evaluation need not disappear; it simply cannot replace every task."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Predictive performance","leftText":"Evaluation of a particular capability concerning future or unseen outcomes.","rightTitle":"The whole value of knowledge","rightText":"Formal proof, textual understanding, and normative reasons have their own burdens of proof."},{"leftTitle":"Local counterexample","leftText":"Challenges particular assumptions, measurements, or object scopes.","rightTitle":"The whole theory necessarily fails","rightText":"Shared premises and dependencies must be examined; the scope cannot expand automatically."},{"leftTitle":"Saying “I agree”","leftText":"May be an actual verbal expression.","rightTitle":"Valid authorization","rightText":"Also requires examination of pressure, information, authority, and applicable institutional conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Epistemic position","leftText":"Affects access to material and the conditions under which speech is trusted.","rightTitle":"Privilege regarding truth","rightText":"Identity alone does not guarantee that every factual claim is correct."},{"leftTitle":"Correcting a factual date","leftText":"Revises a specific factual judgment using reliable material.","rightTitle":"Canceling eligibility to narrate and the meaning of experience","rightText":"There is no inference from a local date error to invalidity of all experience."}],"boundaries":["Excluded people must neither be presumed untrustworthy nor exempted from verification of specific facts.","Understanding language does not replace examining institutional conditions; linguistic differences do not directly establish personality or intelligence problems.","Protective nondisclosure requires credible verification and a corresponding judgment ceiling; it is not immunity for arbitrary assertions.","A valuable new task does not turn an old failed prediction into success; factual and meaning-related outcomes can be handled separately.","This lesson conducted no human interviews or capability assessments, and no full-text historical study of all the listed philosophical works."],"questions":["Why does a local counterexample not always overturn an entire theory?","Does protective nondisclosure mean complete unverifiability?","After correcting the date in a memory, what remains for interpretation of meaning?","Does mutual constraint among methods mean identical theoretical commitments?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-02/figures/section-d-02.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual methodological norm: oral history respects narrators while permitting grounded different conclusions","href":"https://oralhistory.org/oha-statement-on-ethics/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual methodological norm: oral history respects narrators while permitting grounded different conclusions","text":"In the Oral History Association’s 2018 principles, Core Principle 3 explains that memory is shaped by changing personal and social contexts; items 7–9 require attention to power differences, protection from harm, and permission for public use. The final two paragraphs of “Using Oral Sources” in the Statement on Ethics require faithful respect for narrative context while permitting users to reach conclusions different from narrators on accurately cited evidence. Respect, protection, and factual criticism do not exclude one another here.","sourceLabel":"Oral History Association’s official 2018 principles and ethics statement","sourceUrl":"https://oralhistory.org/oha-statement-on-ethics/","sourceLocator":"Core Principles 3 and 7–9; final two paragraphs of “Using Oral Sources” in the Statement on Ethics; “Adopted October 2018” on the main page","boundary":"Only the association’s norms and date were checked for the guide; this does not establish implementation in every interview, nor does adoption of norms make every memory statement true.","href":"https://oralhistory.org/oha-statement-on-ethics/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.02","start_line":4722,"end_line":4737,"startLine":4722,"endLine":4737}],"outputId":"section-d-02","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["First identify whether the current task is formal, empirical, interpretive, normative, or another knowledge activity, and disclose the corresponding grounds.","Judge a counterexample’s effects according to the original proposition’s scope and dependencies. Neither automatically overturn everything nor evade it with unlimited patches.","When changing the research question, preserve old test results; achievements in the new task must not retroactively fill in success for the old one.","Understand speech content and separately check its institutional conditions as a commitment, qualification, or authorizing act.","Improve access to material and safe expression, check facts individually, and preserve experiential meaning; neither disqualify in advance nor grant truth automatically."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Expansion of the source’s conditional example: dates may be corrected while experience still deserves to be heard","text":"Suppose a narrator remembers an event on Tuesday, but a reliable original notice and independent records jointly support Wednesday. The date may be corrected to Wednesday while the account of waiting, fear, or ways of understanding remains material about the corresponding experience. A wrong date does not make every experience fictional, and respect for experience does not justify refusing to correct the date.","assumptions":"A teaching construction expanding the source’s distinction between date and meaning; no real person is involved.","boundary":"Correct the date only when evidence actually supports it; do not assume institutional records are always more reliable than oral accounts."},{"title":"Teaching construction: changing the question cannot repair an old prediction","text":"An imagined team predicts a lower missed-processing rate next month, but it does not fall. Later interviews reveal different understandings of reminders. The interviews can supply material for research on meaning, but do not make the rate prediction correct. Register the original prediction’s outcome and the new research question separately.","assumptions":"The prediction and interview results are hypothetical; no investigation was conducted.","boundary":"The new research still needs qualified material, authorization for participation, and analysis; a changed task does not automatically yield a conclusion."},{"title":"Teaching construction: two levels in a statement of consent","text":"An imagined participant accurately says, “I agree,” but has been told refusal will mean losing necessary support. The expression can be established as occurring, while its words alone cannot establish free and valid authorization. Specific pressure and applicable conditions need examination.","assumptions":"Entirely a teaching construction; no real relationship is judged.","boundary":"No legal ruling is provided; specific validity requires the corresponding institutional and professional material."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"An experiential account contains one wrong date, so the person has no standing to narrate.","effect":"A local factual correction does not cancel subject status or research into experiential meaning.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A disadvantaged position makes everything said inherently true.","effect":"Improving conditions for being heard does not confer privilege regarding truth; verify each claim.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Research produced consensus and was useful, so its factual propositions must be true.","effect":"Consensus, utility, and factual truth are different criteria requiring corresponding material.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does a local counterexample not always overturn an entire theory?","answer":"Examine which assumption it challenges and its dependencies; if it challenges a shared core premise, effects may extend further.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does protective nondisclosure mean complete unverifiability?","answer":"No. Credible reviewers may verify within protective limits, while the strength of conclusions that cannot be publicly supported is restricted.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After correcting the date in a memory, what remains for interpretation of meaning?","answer":"The manner of narration, links to other experiences, and the person’s understanding may retain evidentiary value; correcting a date cannot cancel them together.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does mutual constraint among methods mean identical theoretical commitments?","answer":"No. Preserve their different commitments concerning objects, reasons, experience, and interpretation, and cross-check according to the particular task.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-02/figures/section-d-02.png","alt":"Different knowledge activities—formal, empirical, interpretive, and normative—have their own responsibilities. Counterexamples follow scope and dependencies; a new question ≠ success in an old test. Linguistic meaning and the validity of acts: expressing agreement ≠ valid authorization. Improve epistemic positions through listening, translation, and safety, without exclusion in advance. Verify claims individually, without automatic truth privilege; protection and verification can coexist. Dates may be corrected without erasing narration and meaning. Drawn by the author of this guide from D.02; predictive performance is not the sole standard for all knowledge, nor is identity proof of truth.","caption":"Drawn by the author of this guide from D.02; predictive performance is not the sole standard for all knowledge, nor is identity proof of truth."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Switching between a date fact and experiential meaning could show different evidentiary responsibilities; switching between expression and authorization could display institutional-condition checks.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Actual oral-history methodological norms show that position, protection, and grounded criticism can coexist; no specific interview outcomes are fabricated."},"openQuestions":["Not all original studies indicated by research entries 02.01–02.08 were obtained. The full differences among the listed intellectual traditions and their historical adoption cannot be reconstructed from a directory.","No real interviews were conducted, nor were their conditions for safe expression tested."],"taskId":"S-D-02","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 4545–4560; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["D.02: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"oha-core","title":"OHA Core Principles","url":"https://oralhistory.org/oha-core-principles/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2018 principles; read for the original guide on 2026-10-14","locator":"Items 3, 7, 8, and 9","claim_support":["Memory is shaped by personal and social contexts; attention is required to power, safety, and permission for public use"],"limits":["Only methodological norms were checked, not certification of particular interview facts or implementation effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"oha-ethics","title":"OHA Statement on Ethics","url":"https://oralhistory.org/oha-statement-on-ethics/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2018 principles; read for the original guide on 2026-10-14","locator":"Final two paragraphs of “Using Oral Sources”","claim_support":["Speech and context should be respected faithfully; different conclusions may be proposed on accurately cited evidence"],"limits":["Confers no truth privilege on any account and certifies no actual research quality."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"oha-adoption","title":"OHA Principles and Best Practices","url":"https://oralhistory.org/principles-and-best-practices-revised-2018/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2018-10","locator":"“Adopted October 2018” beneath the title, and the document list","claim_support":["The principles were adopted in October 2018"],"limits":["Version date only; no inference about historical intellectual adoption."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position; Chinese source lines 4631–4646; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-02/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-02/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-02/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-02/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"04473a6faefb2f2016dd01f9b321844fb8830071d3d832defcdcf9ec872110c4","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-02.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-02.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-02.md"};function d(u,e,h,p,m,f){const t=n("SectionLesson");return a(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-02" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.02</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-02" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position {#section-d-02}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §2.1–2.7, §4.8, and §16.6 and 16.15. Research entries cover 02.01–02.08.</p><p>Experience, reasoning, practice, embodied experience, interpretation of texts, and normative argument carry distinct burdens of proof. A formal derivation must disclose its premises and rules. An empirical judgment must show how observations support the claim. Interpretive research must consider the text, context, and competing readings together. Normative argument must state the reasons and positions affected. These forms of inquiry can constrain one another. They cannot all be compressed into one measure of predictive performance.</p><p>Falsification, paradigms, and research programs offer criticism at different scales. A local counterexample may refute an auxiliary hypothesis or require changing the object or measurement. Whether it undermines a broader theory depends on the original claim’s scope and dependencies. A different question can yield valuable new research, but its results cannot be recorded retroactively as success in the old test. Usefulness, actionability, consensus, and truth are distinct standards.</p><p>Language describes and can also constitute action. Making a promise, naming something, granting eligibility, and reporting a fact require different positions from which to speak, rules, and evidence. Someone saying “I consent” may be expressing their wishes sincerely, yet under particular pressures this may be insufficient for valid authorization. Understanding what they mean cannot substitute for checking the institutional conditions. A mismatch in language also cannot serve directly to diagnose personality or intelligence.</p><p>Epistemic position affects who can access material, what questions can be asked, and whose statements are more readily believed. Better conditions for hearing people, translating, and speaking safely can increase available knowledge. Statements by people who are excluded should still be assessed claim by claim. Exclusion neither disqualifies a statement in advance nor grants it an automatic claim to truth. Non-disclosure for protection can coexist with credible verification.</p><p>Methods can correct one another while retaining genuinely different theoretical commitments. The influence of position on knowledge production does not imply that objects can be made up at will. Reasons can enter a causal explanation without removing the separate task of understanding meaning. A reliable record of dates can correct a date someone remembers while preserving their standing to recount the experience and what it meant to them. Recognizing the speaker’s standing does not make every factual proposition automatically true.</p><p>Research topics. 02.01 Empiricism and rationalism (Hume, Descartes, and others). 02.02 Falsification, paradigms, and research programs (Popper, Kuhn, and Lakatos). 02.03 Pragmatism (Peirce, James, and Dewey). 02.04 Realism, constructivism, and critical realism. 02.05 Phenomenology (Husserl, Heidegger, and Merleau-Ponty). 02.06 Hermeneutics (Dilthey, Gadamer, and Ricœur). 02.07 Language, concepts, and context (Wittgenstein, Austin, and others). 02.08 Epistemic injustice and situated knowledge.</p></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"D.02"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=i(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
